+### F13e. Where a 709 → AP1 conversion would be safe, and where it would break
+
+Placing the conversion at the post-chain entry (i.e. on `m_pTexHDRTarget` before any post pass)
+leaves everything upstream untouched — the G-buffer YCC packing (`CommonMath.cfi:138-163`,
+Rec.601 on sqrt values, already not a colorimetric luminance), the shading-time energy
+conservation terms, and `ColorF::Luminance()` on the CPU. Those are safe.
+
+It **breaks** every downstream Rec.709-Y consumer listed in §F13b that operates on linear HDR:
+
+* `HDRPostProcess.cfx:743-753` — auto-exposure metering, which feeds `ComputeExposure`
+  (`:801-816`). An EV shift is guaranteed unless the weights are swapped for AP1's
+  (≈ 0.2722, 0.6741, 0.0537).
+* `HDRPostProcess.cfx:833` — the HDR saturation pivot.
+* `DepthOfField.cfx:435`, `:756`, `:1105-1109` — bokeh highlight thresholding decides *which*
+  highlights become sprites.
+* `PostEffects.cfx:305-363` — firefly suppression would clamp differently.
+* `HDRPostProcess.cfx:168-181` — the streak threshold uses `max(r,g,b)`, which is
+  primaries-dependent too.
+* All of `Clouds.cfx:2804-2815`, `VolumeLighting.cfi:2200-2453` if they run after the conversion
+  point (they run *before* it, so they are safe as long as the conversion is placed after the
+  volumetric composite).
+
